A||||6||chris6878||Upgrading HP on 2.8 V6?
Z||000000||chris6878||08-28-99||12:56 AM||||Hello Everyone....<p>I have recently purchase an 85 GT with a 2.8 V6 and auto trans... the motor is knocking, i'm gonna have to rebuild it any way...  I was wondering in any of you guys (or gals) have have any modifications to recommend  for the 2.8 that would increase horse power<br>Thanks<br>Chris
Z||000001||batboy||08-28-99||06:55 AM||||There are several past discussions on this subject and a current thread going on over in the technical topics. Stroking the engine to a 3.1 is a good start. If that knocking is a rod bearing, then you'll need to rebuild or buy a another crank anyway (probably rods too). Don't skimp on the head work, take them to a good machine shop and expect to pay some money to have them rebuilt right and to make sure they're not cracked. A little pocket porting on the heads will help to improve breathing in most cases. Once the heads breathe better, you have to improve the exhaust too. Headers are expensive for a Fiero, but are worth the money for the power gains. If you are going to stay with the fuel injection and computer, then drop in either the GM hipo cam or the Crane Compucam. Top everything off with nice heavy duty ignition coil, wires, and plugs. Those are the big ticket items that can be done during the rebuild. These upgrades often produce up to 190-200 horsepower, not bad for a 2.8 motor.
Z||000002||chris6878||08-28-99||10:21 AM||||Thanks Batboy,<p>That sounds like some real improvement on the HP. I was thinking of getting the block bored and putting oversized pistions into it.<br>What type of horsepower gain would this achieve? <p>Batboy: You spoke of "If you are going to stay with the fuel injection and computer"<p>What about this? Can you change the Computer and Fuel Injection?<p>Thanks<br>chris<p>
Z||000003||batboy||08-28-99||12:35 PM||||Boring the block doesn't really give you much addition power except that it allows the piston rings to seal up much better which indirectly gives you more power.<p>Some people do away with the fuel injection and buy an aftermarket intake manifold and a carb. You need to find a non-computerized distributor too.
Z||000004||chris6878||08-28-99||06:10 PM||||Are you talking about a "vacum advance" type distributor?<p>Also, when going with the 3.1 crank, would you still use the rods and pistions from the original motor or will they switch out with everything from the 3.1.<p>thanks.<br>chris<p>
Z||000005||Stroker||08-28-99||07:44 PM||||chris6878,<br>The crank will need to come from a 3.1,so will the pistons('90 Camaro for both).The rods are stock 2.8.<p>And yes,you need the older model vac. advance distributor for a carbed setup.<p>(Sorry batboy,I know he was asking you,but I couldn't control myself)<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>Stroker
Z||000006||batboy||08-28-99||08:03 PM||||What Stroker said and also you have to use the right flywheel too.
